over the last 25 years resident evil has  continually changed and evolved as  capcom has tried different mixes of  survival horror and action gameplay  with resident evil 7 capcom swung for  the fences with a first-person  perspective  and more classic horror-focused gameplay  its sequel resident evil village  evolves that idea to make something that  feels very different from its  predecessor  but which is just as engaging though the  perspective and the mechanical  underpinnings are the same  village branches off in its own  direction from re7  capturing some of the things that were  great about that game while resisting  the impulse to retread the same ground  while it's still frightening at points  it takes a less horror-driven tack  on the same formula village continues to  evolve resident evil  while maintaining a keen grasp on some  of its core tenets  finding new ways or reviving old ones of  getting under your skin and ratcheting  up the tension  where the hell am i  [Music]  as has been pretty clear for a while now  resident evil village is resident evil 7  through the lens of resident evil 4.  when the latter was released back in  2005  it totally revamped what the franchise  had been up to that point  swapping the earlier game slower  survival horror focus  for a more fast-paced action approach  trading darkened corridors and jump  scares  for adrenaline-fueled panic village adds  to re7  by taking its cues from the faster  panickier re4  it's again played from a tight closed in  first person perspective  and it still often focuses on slower  movement and exploration  but village's approach is distinctly  more action-centric  and it's remarkable how much capcom has  managed to push the underlying formula  of its reboot to the series  in such a different direction  village picks up on resident evil 7's  story three years later  again focusing on protagonist ethan  winters after rescuing his wife mia from  the baker house  and subsequently getting saved by  resident evil franchise mainstay chris  redfield  ethan and mia went into hiding with  chris's help moving to europe to restart  their lives  they've since had a daughter but their  lives are abandoned again when chris and  his team bust into the couple's house  and drag ethan and baby rose off ethan  wakes up sometime later after a car  crash  the two random special forces guys who  were transporting him and rose are dead  ethan wanders through the snowy woods in  search of his missing child  until he hits a ramshackle seemingly  empty village before long  he discovers it's under siege by what  appeared to be werewolves  it's all extremely reminiscent of the  beginning of resident evil 4  which upended the ari formula by  dropping you into a village overrun with  enemies  who acted like humans rather than  shambling mindless zombies  the likens you face wield weapons and  shoot arrows at you  and to avoid getting surrounded you can  run into houses and barricade the doors  with furniture to slow their advances  just like its 2005 inspiration the  opening battle of village taps into a  frightening intensity of trying to  create a defensible position  or take part in a running gunfight that  you're in serious danger of losing  it's a completely different feeling from  the slow burn dread of re7  but village's combat can make your heart  pound just as hard  the fact that village feels like such a  turn away from re7  is what works about it as a sequel it  feels like capcom reaching for a new way  to challenge itself  most of the time in village at least on  its standard difficulty  you'll find yourself pretty well  outfitted for whatever you're about to  face  the challenges in using those resources  effectively and keeping yourself alive  village uses the same movement system as  re7 which can feel a little slow and  clunky at times  eden isn't especially agile and even at  a sprint  it can feel like he's barely moving  despite a sense of trudging through mud  village is tuned so that enemies also  approach slowly and cautiously  so dealing with them comes together like  a standoff that has you judging their  moves  or hitting their weak points before they  have a chance to get in close  and rip into you and sometimes you just  have to run and hope you can find a  better place to make your stand  while the controls add tension when  you're surrounded they can be a bit  obtuse making it difficult to avoid  attacks  you're often dodging attacks as a  monster charges you or swings at you  with a huge weapon  you can absorb some damage with the  ability to hold up your hands in a guard  another re7 feature that helps mitigate  the limitations of your movement  but the better strategy is to try to run  around the battlefield and get out of  the way  that usually means sacrificing seeing  what your opponent is doing  since ethan can't step sideways while  looking forward you're constantly just  running and hoping whatever is coming at  you  doesn't hit you it doesn't make these  battles frustrating or add to their  difficulty  but it does add a counter-intuitiveness  to combat that can take you out of the  moment  bullets are easy to come by and if  you're short you can craft more from  materials you find  or purchase them from the duke a  traveling merchant all  that stuff you're carrying around has to  go somewhere so village brings back the  inventory management system of re4  you have a suitcase full of weapons ammo  and health items with each taking up  space on a grid  you can move items around to make sure  they all fit in your case tetris  style and purchase bigger cases from the  duke so you can bring more stuff  the system is most interesting when it  starts making you wonder if you should  sell off old guns or toss less useful  items  in favor of things like extra ammo  mercifully  crafting items don't count against your  inventory space so you're free to make  bullets and health items on the fly  on standard difficulty it was never a  big concern but you do have to start  thinking about keeping space open as you  explore  in case you find something essential  though village has far more variety of  enemies to fight than re7  it's not all shooting with a lot of  varied pacing and new challenges  throughout  there are four different areas to unlock  as you progress through the story  each headed by one of the four lords the  scary monster people who dominate the  village  and the area around it after the opening  village the first is the castle run by  lady dimitresk  where there's a lot less shooting to be  done you'll occasionally face enemies  you have to gun down  but much more time in the castle is  spent navigating its tight corridors  as you're hunted by the vampire  demotrask and her daughters  you can't kill the vampires with your  conventional weapons so you have to run  if you're found  the entire level is spent exploring the  castle looking for the items you need to  advance through the area  while listening for dimatresk's clumping  footsteps and trying to keep away from  her  even in this first new area village is  mixing and remixing its experience  with different horror ideas you're not  just shotgunning a ton of enemies  you're also engaging in stealth more  akin to what navigating the baker house  was like in re7  and later portions of the game throw  different obstacles into your path  there are areas that are full of puzzles  that are reminiscent of the mansion of  resident evil  or the police station of re2 there are  sections where you hear frightfully  powerful enemies in the distance you  know you'll have to fight  and there are points that show village  can still hone in on some of the intense  frights that made re7  so remarkable suffice to say that  village is great at maintaining an  atmosphere of unease  as you head around each new corner sure  you have plenty of weapons  but can you handle what you're about to  face will they even work against  the next creature you encounter there's  an excellent balance between providing  lots of action  and maintaining that dread village  encourages its own kind of fear  and hits you with a few great re7 like  set pieces  to ratchet up the tension when you least  expect it no two sections of village are  quite the same  each draws on different aspects of past  resident evil games and combines them  with that underlying  re7 feel to create something that's both  new and nostalgic  it's impressive how the game can change  to be a little different  again and again throughout its run time  mixing in other ideas from the franchise  to make each section  fun intense and naturally frightening in  its own way  the exception to that is the last hour  of the game where things take a weird  over-the-top turn in the run-up to  village's final moments  the action gets turned up even higher it  seems like a needless digression  where village draws on the worst  action-heavy portions of resident evil 6  losing track of what made you grit your  teeth and clench your fists  and exchanging it for something more  akin to shooters like call of duty  this same moment highlights some of the  inconsistency in village's story  though the game's imagery recalls the  umbrella logo from past games  village's connection to the rest of the  franchise feels a bit tacked on  the same is true of chris redfield whose  presence in the story  feels like an afterthought ethan's  interactions with the four lords and the  mystery of the village  is more fun and interesting since the  antagonists come off as petty  flawed and human they don't particularly  like each other  but are all eager to torture and maim  you anyway  and their interactions with ethan and  each other add dimension to their  characters  likens and gentlemen  we thank you and now let the games begin  village is a spooky romp with fun  villains but it's just a shame that more  time wasn't devoted to the hanging story  threads  of re7 or the franchise at large  thankfully  that digression doesn't last long  meanwhile the elements about combat that  are fun  are transplanted into the fast-paced  arcade mode  the mercenaries the mode takes the  combat of the village  slaps a timer on it and focuses it on  scoring points  the mercenaries is a fun side activity  in its own right  always daring you to get a higher score  by balancing taking down as many enemies  as you can  and trying to complete one of its given  levels as fast as you can  it demonstrates how tight the combat and  village can be  although at times it can be held back a  bit by the slow underwater feeling of  movement around the battlefield  resident evil 7 was an excellent return  to the horror roots of the franchise  with some coming new ideas similarly  village is an intelligent reimagination  of the best action elements  of resident evil though it captures some  of the same things that made re7  such a breath of fresh air village uses  new takes on the ideas of the past  to evolve into its own unique creature
 

